## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a policy-driven, per-tenant response shaping mechanism in a docker-dca setup, using a Config object propagated to the service. It demonstrates practical data-safety concerns and integration with Traefik TLS termination.

## Key Concepts

- Docker Config for policy data
- Per-tenant response shaping and JSON filtering
- Payload transformation without altering business logic
- Traefik TLS edge in multi-DC deployments
- Basic end-to-end testing in a docker-dca context

## Code Example

```go
// Go example: filterFields filters payload by allowed keys
func filterFields(in map[string]interface{}, allowed []string) map[string]interface{} {
    out := make(map[string]interface{})
    set := make(map[string]struct{}, len(allowed))
    for _, k := range allowed { set[k] = struct{}{} }
    for k, v := range in {
        if _, ok := set[k]; ok {
            out[k] = v
        }
    }
    return out
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for a tenant with no allowed fields? What would the response look like?
- What are the security implications if the tenant config is mis-synced between DCs, and how would you detect/mitigate it?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests depth of cross-DC security, dynamic policy loading, and automated risk controls in a real-world Docker DC architecture.

## Key Concepts

- SPIFFE/SVIDs with Vault
- WASM-based per-tenant policy engine
- Edge verifier sidecar for SVID validation
- Canary deployments and automatic rollback
- Cross-DC routing and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: load tenant policy from broker and validate SVID before calling app
async function handleRequest(req){
  const svid = await verifySVID(req.headers['spiffe']);
  const(policy) = await loadPolicyFromBroker(req.tenant);
  if(!policy.allows(req)) throw new Error('Access denied');
  return proxyToApp(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you debug a scenario where Vault rotation invalidates existing SVIDs mid-rollout?
- What metrics and traces would you collect to enforce the 25% canary SLA thresholds across DCs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design distributed feature-flag propagation with per-tenant isolation across data centers. It covers Docker Config for tenant flags, per-tenant in-process caching with TTL, Redis Pub/Sub for invalidation and cross-DC propagation, and edge security with TLS termination at Traefik.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for tenant-specific flag storage
- Per-tenant in-process cache with configurable TTL
- Redis Pub/Sub for cache invalidation and propagation
- Lightweight policy/flag service coordinating across DCs
- TLS termination at Traefik edge proxy

## Code Example
```javascript
class FlagCache {
  constructor(redis, ttlSeconds = 120) {
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.ttlSeconds = ttlSeconds;
    this.redis = redis;

    redis.subscribe('tenant-flags', (err, count) => {
      if (err) console.error('Subscribe error:', err);
    });

    redis.on('message', (channel, message) => {
      if (channel === 'tenant-flags') {
        const { tenantId } = JSON.parse(message);
        this.cache.delete(tenantId);
      }
    });
  }

  async getFlag(tenantId, feature) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${feature}`;
    const entry = this.cache.get(key);

    if (entry && Date.now() < entry.expiresAt) {
      return entry.value;
    }

    const value = await this.redis.hget(`flags:${tenantId}`, feature);
    this.cache.set(key, { value: JSON.parse(value), expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlSeconds * 1000 });
    return JSON.parse(value);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical skills in dynamic per-tenant API version routing, assessing candidates' knowledge of Redis-backed routing state, Traefik dynamic configuration, and Docker-based edge proxies across distributed infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant version routing
- Redis-backed configuration store (tenant_id → api_version)
- Traefik dynamic configuration via Docker provider
- Sidecar pattern for pushing configuration updates
- Multi-datacenter consistency and TLS edge handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sidecar that watches Redis and updates Traefik rules
async function syncRoutes(redis, docker) {
  const routes = await redis.hgetall('tenant:versions');
  for (const [tenantId, version] of Object.entries(routes)) {
    await updateTraefikRule(docker, tenantId, version);
  }
}
```
